<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minh Hương</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Minh Huong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Minh Hương were descendants of Chinese, many of them Ming loyalists, who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> in 1644. Registered as a separate community, they intermarried with Vietnamese, adopted local customs and served as merchants, officials and scholars under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a> and emperors. The scholar-official Trịnh Hoài Đức was among them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minh-huong.jpg" alt="Minh Hương" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bùi Thụy Đào Nguyên · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:B%C3%AAn_trong_%C4%90%C3%ACnh_Minh_H%C6%B0%C6%A1ng_Gia_Th%E1%BA%A1nh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
